Sorting makes all equal values sit next to each other. Since the required value appears more than half the time, it must cover the middle index after sorting. The answer is therefore `nums[n / 2]`.

This changes the input order, so it is not a good choice when the original array must stay unchanged. A one-element array also works because index `0` is the middle index.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int majorityElement(vector<int>& nums) {
        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());
        return nums[nums.size() / 2];
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` for sorting.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space apart from the sorting implementation.

Only a candidate and a balance are needed. Matching values add one vote, while different values remove one vote. When the balance reaches zero, the next value becomes the new candidate.

Dry run for `[2, 2, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2]`: the first two `2` values raise the balance to `2`. The next two `1` values cancel that lead. The later values leave `2` with the final balance, so `2` is returned.

This does not change the input array and uses constant extra space. It works because the majority value has more occurrences than every other value combined.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int majorityElement(vector<int>& nums) {
        int candidate = 0;
        int balance = 0;

        for (int number : nums) {
            if (balance == 0) {
                candidate = number;
            }

            if (number == candidate) {
                balance++;
            } else {
                balance--;
            }
        }

        return candidate;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` with one pass through the array.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

A direct version is useful for checking the idea. For every number, the code scans the whole array and counts how often that number appears. As soon as the count is more than half of the array, it returns that number.

For example, with `[3, 2, 3]`, the first `3` gets a count of `2`, which is greater than `3 / 2`.

```java
class Solution {
    public int majorityElement(int[] nums) {
        int limit = nums.length / 2;

        for (int number : nums) {
            int count = 0;

            for (int value : nums) {
                if (value == number) {
                    count++;
                }
            }

            if (count > limit) {
                return number;
            }
        }

        return 0;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because every number may scan the full array.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

The array is split into two parts until each part has one value. Each half returns its possible majority candidate. If both candidates match, that value is kept. Otherwise, the code counts both candidates in the current range and keeps the one with the larger count.

For an odd-sized range, the middle index calculation still divides the range correctly. The problem guarantees that a majority exists, so the final candidate is safe to return.

```java
class Solution {
    public int majorityElement(int[] nums) {
        return findMajority(nums, 0, nums.length - 1);
    }

    private int findMajority(int[] nums, int left, int right) {
        if (left == right) {
            return nums[left];
        }

        int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
        int leftCandidate = findMajority(nums, left, middle);
        int rightCandidate = findMajority(nums, middle + 1, right);

        if (leftCandidate == rightCandidate) {
            return leftCandidate;
        }

        int leftCount = 0;
        int rightCount = 0;

        for (int index = left; index <= right; index++) {
            if (nums[index] == leftCandidate) {
                leftCount++;
            }
            if (nums[index] == rightCandidate) {
                rightCount++;
            }
        }

        return leftCount > rightCount ? leftCandidate : rightCandidate;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` because each recursion level counts across its ranges.
Space complexity: `O(log n)` for the recursive call stack.

A dictionary keeps the count for each value while the array is scanned. There is no need for a second pass. When one count becomes larger than `len(nums) // 2`, that value is the answer.

Dry run for `[2, 2, 1, 2]`: the counts become `2: 1`, then `2: 2`, then `1: 1`, and finally `2: 3`. The limit is `2`, so the last update returns `2`.

The map uses more memory than the voting method, but the counting rule is easy to follow.

```python
class Solution:
    def majorityElement(self, nums):
        counts = {}
        limit = len(nums) // 2

        for number in nums:
            counts[number] = counts.get(number, 0) + 1
            if counts[number] > limit:
                return number

        return 0
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` on average with dictionary operations.
Space complexity: `O(n)` in the case where most values are different.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution {
public:
    int majorityElement(vector<int>& nums) {
        int count = 0;
        int compare = 0;
        for(int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            if(count == 0) {
                compare = nums[i];
            }
            if(nums[i] == compare) {
                count++;
            } else {
                count--;
            }      
        }
        return compare;
    }
};
```

## Intuition
The majority element is the element that appears more than n / 2 times in the array.

The key observation is that instead of comparing every element with every other element, we can keep track of how many times each number has appeared using a HashMap.

As soon as the frequency of any number becomes greater than n / 2, we know that it is the majority element and can return it immediately.

## Approach
1. Find the majority threshold by calculating nums.length / 2.
2.Create a HashMap where:
-        Key = array element
-        Value = number of times that element has appeared.
3. Traverse the array using a for-each loop.
4. For every element, get its current frequency from the map.
5. Increase the frequency by 1.
6. If the updated frequency becomes greater than n / 2, return that element immediately.
7. If no majority element is found, return -1.
8. The single-element array is handled separately because that element is automatically the majority element.

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
  public int majorityElement(int[] nums) {
    if (nums.length == 1) {
      return nums[0];
    }

    int max = nums.length / 2;
    HashMap<Integer, Integer> occurrencesMap = new HashMap<>();

    for (int num : nums) {
      int count = occurrencesMap.getOrDefault(num, 0) + 1;
      if (count > max) {
        return num;
      }

      occurrencesMap.put(num, count);
    }
    return -1;
  }
}
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:**
O(n) — We traverse the array once. HashMap insertion and lookup take O(1) average time

**Space Complexity:**
O(n) — In the worst case, every element is different, so the HashMap can contain n distinct elements.7f:T

